  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how electric polarization can arise from electronic motion in topological or lattice-based systems. In the upper left, an energy–momentum band diagram shows two symmetric bands and several highlighted momentum points. Red arrows inside faint ellipses indicate the direction of an internal texture, likely showing how a momentum-dependent electronic quantity changes across the Brillouin zone. The overall message is that opposite sides of the band structure carry differently oriented contributions.\n\nThe upper right presents a 2D electronic bulk with circulating blue loops, while a narrow strip at the boundary is labeled as 1D electronic edge states. External electric and magnetic fields are applied, suggesting a field-driven response that links bulk orbital-like circulation to conductive edge behavior.\n\nThe bottom row uses a simple lattice of positive and negative sites to explain polarization \\(P\\). In the left example, the local arrangement is symmetric, so \\(P \\approx 0\\). In the middle and right examples, the central charge shifts relative to its neighbors, producing polarization along either the vertical or horizontal direction. Together, the panels connect microscopic charge displacement and momentum-space texture to macroscopic polarization and edge-state phenomena in low-dimensional electronic materials.",
